      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "With respect to allergy, the possibility of cross-reactivity between snail and mite is well recognized, and anecdotal reports suggesting that allergen immunotherapy with mite extract can worsen snail-induced allergy exist."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "We describe the effect of immunotherapy in 4 children with snail-mite allergy."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Four children (1 boy and 3 girls; 9-13 years of age) had consistent clinical histories (mild immediate respiratory symptoms after ingestion) and positive skin reactions for allergy to snail. They also had mite-induced asthma and were therefore prescribed subcutaneous specific immunotherapy and subsequently followed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Several months (8-25) after starting immunotherapy, all children experienced life-threatening reactions, anaphylaxis, and respiratory failure after inadvertent ingestion of snail. Skin reactivity to the fresh food increased in all patients."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "This observation confirms that in patients with combined mite-snail allergy, immunotherapy should be avoided."
        }
      ]},
